        "html": "<p>The preceding chapters established who belongs to a population and how counted events can be compared fairly. But once people began finding regularities in births and deaths, a new difficulty came into view: a population rate describes what happened; it cannot tell us with certainty what will happen next. In sixteenth-century Italy a more tightly bounded version of that difficulty was already being debated around games of chance. Consider a wager on two dice: one player backs a total of <strong><bdi dir=\"ltr\">2</bdi></strong>, another backs <strong><bdi dir=\"ltr\">7</bdi></strong>, and both are offered the same prize. Each has selected one number. Are the wagers equally fair?</p>\n<p>The difficulty is not that the players lack skill in arithmetic. They can add six and one as readily as they can add one and one. Their problem is that ordinary arithmetic describes <strong>what has happened</strong>, while their agreement concerns <strong>what has not yet happened</strong>. No one knows the next throw. Yet money must be committed now. Is there a reasonable way to compare two uncertain possibilities before either occurs?</p>\n<p>This comparison captures the kind of difficulty that preoccupied writers on games of chance; it is not presented as a recorded Cardano wager. Dice and cards, which might appear an unlikely birthplace for serious mathematics, were especially useful precisely because their rules could be described. In a world of unpredictable illness, weather, trade, and war, it was hard even to list everything that might happen. With dice, the uncertainty remained real, but its boundaries were unusually clear.</p>\n<p>Arguments about gambling also had a moral dimension. A wager could be lawful or unlawful, prudent or reckless, equitable or exploitative. A player might ask not just <em>Will I win?</em> but <em>Am I being offered a just exchange for the risk I accept?</em> Gerolamo Cardano (1501–1576)—physician, mathematician, prolific author, and gambler—examined such questions in <em>Liber de ludo aleae</em>, usually translated as <em>The Book on Games of Chance</em>. Historian David Bellhouse has shown why it is misleading to read the work as merely an embryonic modern probability textbook: notions of justice, knowledge, and Renaissance ethical thought help explain its organization and purpose <sup><a href=\"#chance-source-1\" aria-label=\"Source H1\">[H1]</a></sup>.</p>\n<p>Cardano was not alone, and the problem did not originate with him. Medieval texts had enumerated dice combinations; Renaissance writers debated how to divide a stake when a game ended prematurely. Cardano's importance lies in the relatively systematic way he connected possible cases, game rules, and the evaluation of chances. Even the date of his work resists a tidy origin story. Scholars have proposed different dates for its composition and revisions, including dates much earlier than 1564; its first printing came only in <strong><bdi dir=\"ltr\">1663</bdi></strong>, long after his death <sup><a href=\"#chance-source-2\" aria-label=\"Source H2\">[H2]</a> <a href=\"#chance-source-3\" aria-label=\"Source H3\">[H3]</a> <a href=\"#chance-source-4\" aria-label=\"Source H4\">[H4]</a></sup>. We should distinguish an idea being <strong>written</strong>, being <strong>published</strong>, and actually <strong>influencing</strong> later thinkers. Those three dates need not coincide.</p>\n<div data-chance-gallery></div>\n<p>The wager can now be returned to the table. The players have chosen the totals 2 and 7. The tempting argument is that each result is one of eleven possible sums, from 2 through 12, so their chances should be equal. But the apparent symmetry is false. <strong>The same named result can be reached in different numbers of ways.</strong> Discovering which ways ought to be counted is the decisive mathematical step.</p>"
